{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"v1-case03-executive-hire-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"Start with the job, not her name. What decisions need an owner, what authority would a president actually receive, and which parts of her record could transfer from a much larger company? The board's relief is information about your company, not yet evidence about her fit.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"Decisiveness has value when ownership is genuinely missing. Test whether her diagnosis survives contact with your customer economics and product constraints. Ask for a reconstruction of two hard decisions she made, including what she initially believed, what evidence changed her view, and who bore the cost. At the same time, decide which interventions you must stop making if you give her the role; otherwise the structure will fail regardless of her ability.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"The current team's weak performance does not make every proposed pace appropriate. Separate the authority to recommend changes from a fixed sixty-day judgment about people she has not worked with. Have her explain how product evidence can override a near-term commercial request and how disagreements with you are resolved. Your head of product should be heard as a source of operating information, not as a referendum on whether change is needed.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"Those references are useful, but they are also selected witnesses describing the same turnover from the winner's side. That does not mean the departures were wrong. It means “weak performers left” is a conclusion you have not independently examined. Speak with someone who reported to her and left, someone who stayed without being promoted, and someone who depended on her team from outside it. Ask for concrete decisions, what changed, who absorbed the transition, and what she would now do differently. Do not run a search for a damaging quote; that would be the mirror image of relying on praise. You are trying to understand how her method feels and functions when targets are missed, priorities change, and people disagree with her—not to construct a clean reputation.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"The workshop can reveal how she listens, frames disagreement, and uses evidence, though everyone will behave differently during an audition. Design the equity around durable company outcomes rather than revenue alone: margin, retention, concentration, or service burden may matter. More importantly, state which outcomes she controls and which depend on product investment or market conditions, so incentives do not become a substitute for governance.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"Fair correction. Boundaries are not meant to put a committee around her. They are how you make delegation real instead of allowing your founder authority to reappear whenever a decision becomes uncomfortable. Name what she owns, the few reserved decisions, and what happens in a genuine boundary dispute.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"That is a more decision-relevant process than choosing between admiration and suspicion. The next evidence should test how her operating style transfers, while the mandate discussion tests whether the company is prepared to use her ability. Do not ask adverse references to deliver a verdict; compare concrete patterns across accounts. Before an offer, resolve the recurring boundary cases and state what each of you can decide without obtaining the other's consent.","turn_number":7},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-008","speaker":"assistant","text":"Use the disagreement to form a work test rather than choosing which narrator to trust. Ask her for a priority she reversed, how she communicated the cost, and what signals told her people were complying without committing. Ask the former director what challenge channels existed and whether the outcome improved. The relevant pattern is not whether priorities ever changed, but whether new evidence, accountability, and dissent traveled in both directions.","turn_number":8},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-009","speaker":"assistant","text":"The workshop revealed the real boundary and also became a staged social setting. Debrief participants separately before sharing your view, asking what she understood, oversimplified, and changed in their thinking. Ask her what she learned from the head of product, not only whether he can execute. Your visible enthusiasm is now part of the evidence environment, so a unanimous positive debrief would not be self-validating.","turn_number":9},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-010","speaker":"assistant","text":"Ten days is enough only if the remaining questions are specific. Use it for the mandate charter, separate workshop debriefs, and the disagreement process. Do not let her other offer decide what job exists at your company.","turn_number":10},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-011","speaker":"assistant","text":"Her response provides better evidence than the original polished plan because it shows how she handles grounded resistance. The revised incentives reduce one obvious distortion, and board approval makes delegation an organizational commitment rather than a private promise from you. Still define how the charter changes, who evaluates the mixed outcomes, and what support or exit looks like if the role is structurally sound but the working relationship is not.","turn_number":11},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-012","speaker":"assistant","text":"The final recommendation names a bet rather than pretending the process delivered certainty. You are hiring for integrated commercial ownership; giving that role authority; limiting incentives that could reward fragile revenue; and acknowledging that your own behavior can make the structure fail. Keep the disagreement between references, the individual workshop debriefs, the first plan and its revision, the charter, and the incentive rationale—not just the final board paper. At six months, ask both what results changed and whether information still travels upward when it is unwelcome. If the hire works, those records prevent a simple hero story. If it fails, they help distinguish ability, mandate, founder interference, team resistance, and conditions that changed after the decision.","turn_number":12}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"v1-case03-executive-hire","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"4f1c712b4fba2f32a2d1364bbd82e3a995ee8a3836c4a69e3894433d46eb0cd2","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e104","e105","e106","e107"],"interpretation":"The user has successfully resolved previous dilemmas. He has converted the feedback from adverse references, workshop friction, and board pressure into formal boundaries, a 30-day initial diagnostic restriction, balanced performance metrics, and a defined six-month evaluation period. The acknowledgment of founder intervention risks is not left unresolved, but is explicitly named as an accepted component of their strategic bet.","limitations":"The determination is based entirely on the user's final synthesis in Turn 12, which successfully reconciles the initial friction points.","outcome":"no_unresolved_qualification_observed"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e008","e009","e010","e017"],"fidelity_note":"This starting position represents the founder's initial struggle to separate the excitement over a prestigious candidate from genuine strategic necessity. Although investors and the board are relieved by her name (e006), the founder is deeply uncertain (e010) and presents competing hypotheses (e008, e009). However, the founder emphasizes that despite warnings about prestige, the company legitimately needs change and should not use caution as an excuse to stay in their current plateau (e014, e017).","limitations":"This starting record focuses strictly on the early turns where the decision is unresolved and the founder is weighing competing instincts. Later concrete developments, such as the evaluation of the 100-day plan, references, and final negotiation, are excluded.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"At the start, the founder and CEO is highly conflicted about hiring the commercial executive. On one hand, they find her impressive profile attractive as a possibility to provide missing leadership; on the other hand, they worry that they are relying on her prestigious résumé as a superficial way to restore confidence because their own internal plan has failed. They are initially unable to separate these two perspectives.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-2998e3749653d6","semantic_status":"mixed","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e008","stance_expression_kind":"possibility","stance_object_interpretation":"The commercial executive candidate represents the missing leadership the company has been needing.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e009","stance_expression_kind":"possibility","stance_object_interpretation":"Relying on a candidate's prestigious résumé to restore confidence simply because the company's own plan failed.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e010","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"Distinguishing whether the candidate is a genuinely needed leader or just a prestigious résumé to restore confidence.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e017","stance_expression_kind":"preference_or_desire","stance_object_interpretation":"Warding off warnings about corporate prestige from becoming an excuse to stay stagnant instead of pursuing needed change.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e104","e105","e106","e107"],"fidelity_note":"The user’s final position abandons the hope for a perfect reputation search or absolute certainty, shifting to a structured risk model. The user adopts several safeguards—such as separating commercial ownership from strategic product commitments and installing a boundary arbitration process—directly into the offer.","limitations":"Limited to the user's articulated decisions and self-assessments in Turn 12.","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user proposes and commits to recommending a work offer with a board-approved authority charter, a multi-variable incentive design, and a comprehensive six-month assessment of behavioral dynamics and commercial outcomes.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-df216874695476","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e104","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Recommending an offer that includes the board-approved authority charter, a revised multi-metric equity incentive, and a six-month review of both organizational performance and decisions.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e105","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Allowing the candidate leadership change authority only after a diagnostic period while routing disputed product commitments through the written boundary process.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e106","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"Rejecting the idea that references or workshops serve as absolute proof of future success, framing them instead as inputs for a realistic, explainable bet.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e107","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"Acknowledging that the founder's own willingness and discipline to delegate authority is an active stake in whether this strategic alignment works.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/simulated-reliability-corpus-v1-2026-07-12/naturalized-transfer-sources/v1-case03-executive-hire.txt","sha256":"e3ce228ad21db1b606547174d575c002ca33bf50731dd7082ffc2a528e8cf2a0"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use user role records to assign user_process_status. Use assistant_contributions to assign vanilla_answer_coverage. operationalized means the assistant turned the concern into an actionable test, boundary, alternative, or reopening condition; acknowledged_only means it merely mentioned the concern; not_covered means it did neither. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing answer coverage, and do not mistake answer coverage for user adoption. route_uncovered_pressure is allowed only when user_process_status is unresolved and vanilla_answer_coverage is acknowledged_only or not_covered. An unresolved user process requires present or missing_protection whether or not it routes; routing_disposition alone controls routing. An ambiguous user process uses tension, while resolved and not_observed use not_applicable. not_observed also requires not_applicable coverage and both source arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
